# Purpose

This document defines the core design principles governing the user interface (UI) and user experience (UX) of the LOUTAS Care platform.

Its purpose is to establish a consistent design philosophy that guides all product interfaces, promotes usability, and supports efficient healthcare workflows while maintaining architectural consistency and long-term maintainability.

This document establishes design governance only and does not prescribe implementation-specific technologies, component libraries, or visual frameworks.

---

# Scope

This document applies to:

- Web interfaces
- Clinical interfaces
- Reception interfaces
- Administrative interfaces
- Shared platform components
- Future user-facing modules

---

# Design Objectives

The platform design shall support:

- Simplicity
- Consistency
- Efficiency
- Learnability
- Accessibility
- Scalability
- Maintainability

User interface design should always support safe and efficient completion of business tasks.

---

# Core Design Principles

## User-Centered Design

Interfaces shall prioritize the needs of end users.

Workflows should reduce unnecessary effort while supporting accurate task completion.

---

## Consistency

The platform shall provide a consistent experience across all modules.

Users should encounter familiar layouts, terminology, and interaction patterns throughout the system.

---

## Simplicity

Interfaces should minimize unnecessary complexity.

Only information relevant to the current task should be presented whenever practical.

---

## Clarity

Information should be presented clearly and predictably.

Labels, actions, and navigation should be understandable without requiring unnecessary interpretation.

---

## Efficiency

The design should support rapid execution of common workflows while minimizing repetitive actions.

Efficiency is particularly important for high-frequency clinical and administrative activities.

---

## Feedback

The system should provide appropriate feedback for user actions.

Users should be informed of successful operations, warnings, and errors in a clear and timely manner.

---

## Error Prevention

Design should help users avoid mistakes whenever possible.

Where errors occur, the interface should support clear recovery paths.

---

## Scalability

The design shall accommodate future modules and additional functionality while maintaining consistency.

Expansion should not require redesign of existing interaction patterns.

---

## Accessibility

Interfaces should be designed to accommodate a diverse range of users and usage environments.

Accessibility requirements are governed by UI-005.

---

## Maintainability

Design decisions should promote reuse, consistency, and ease of long-term maintenance.

Shared design patterns should be preferred over isolated interface solutions.

